WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.448
Communication is where intelligence work becomes decision support.

00:00:03.448 --> 00:00:07.633
A strong product separates facts, assumptions, analytic judgments, confidence, caveats,

00:00:07.633 --> 00:00:09.307
and recommended next steps.

00:00:09.307 --> 00:00:15.165
That separation is especially important across jurisdictions because different audiences may have different authorities,

00:00:15.165 --> 00:00:16.839
risks, and information needs.

00:00:16.839 --> 00:00:19.769
The same intelligence may need different versions.

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:24.372
Executives may need business impact, confidence, recommended action, and decisions required.

00:00:24.372 --> 00:00:28.557
Engineers may need affected systems, indicators, timeframes, and operational caveats.

00:00:28.557 --> 00:00:33.160
Legal and privacy reviewers may need source, authority, data categories, retention,

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:34.416
and dissemination history.

00:00:34.416 --> 00:00:39.019
External partners may need a sanitized summary with clear handling markings.

00:00:39.019 --> 00:00:44.459
Giving every audience the same raw package can create confusion and unnecessary exposure.

00:00:44.459 --> 00:00:46.133
Confidence language prevents overclaiming.

00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:51.155
Analysts should say what is observed, what is assessed, what is unknown,

00:00:51.155 --> 00:00:53.666
and what would change the assessment.

00:00:53.666 --> 00:00:57.014
Caveats should be visible: preliminary, limited telemetry, partner-provided,

00:00:57.014 --> 00:01:00.780
not for onward sharing, sanitized, time-bound, or pending review.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:05.802
Clear caveats help decision-makers use intelligence without treating it as more certain

00:01:05.802 --> 00:01:08.313
or more shareable than it is.

00:01:08.313 --> 00:01:11.242
Escalation paths should be defined before pressure.

00:01:11.242 --> 00:01:15.427
Urgent risk, privacy concern, legal uncertainty, cross-border sharing, public reporting,

00:01:15.427 --> 00:01:17.938
sensitive partner information, potential employee impact,

00:01:17.938 --> 00:01:20.449
or executive action may require escalation.

00:01:20.449 --> 00:01:25.470
The playbook or operating routine should identify who can approve next steps,

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:29.655
who provides backup review, and which channel should be used.

00:01:29.655 --> 00:01:31.748
After-action review closes the loop.

00:01:31.748 --> 00:01:36.769
Teams should ask what worked, what slowed coordination, what policy gaps appeared,

00:01:36.769 --> 00:01:40.536
which data sources were missing, whether caveats were understood,

00:01:40.536 --> 00:01:44.302
whether sharing was appropriate, and which records need improvement.

00:01:44.302 --> 00:01:48.487
Reviews should update templates, requirements, partner lists, handling guidance, detections,

00:01:48.487 --> 00:01:49.742
and escalation paths.

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:52.253
Good communication is precise and modest.

00:01:52.253 --> 00:01:55.601
It avoids spy-fiction language, speculation, and unsupported conclusions.

00:01:55.601 --> 00:01:56.856
It respects confidentiality.

00:01:56.856 --> 00:02:01.878
It tells each audience what they need to know, why it matters,

00:02:01.878 --> 00:02:06.900
how confident the team is, and what responsible action is recommended next.
